<blockquote>
Shopping Around: Pre-Release Musings<br />
Written by Mayhem<br />
<br />
Welcome to the first edition of our Shopping Around series of articles.  In
this issue, we'll be taking a look at some things to consider as a "normal"
player who may be considering moving into the Everquest 2 game.  After all,
only one person in fifty or so will be a guild leader, so the other 49 need
to figure out which of those guilds to join!<br />
<br />
The first decision you need to make is when you're interested in joining a
guild.  This is not really something which is hard and fast, as the decision
to join can be made when appropriate, of course.  You may however have
leanings towards one of the options below.<br />
<ul>
<li>
Joining a guild long in advance of release, working with the leader and
other members to plan for a strong presence at release.
</li>
<li>
Waiting until shortly before release in order to see what guilds are
available at that point so as to have a larger pool of available choices.
</li>
<li>
Joining a guild shortly after release, thus insuring that the guild you join
is "official" and active in the game.
</li>
<li>
Waiting until after release to join a guild with members you know from
in-game which seems to suit you best.
</li>
</ul>
Personally, I have experience with three of these (in a way) and have seen
the benefits of each.  The only choice I have not taken is the first, as I
have never been involved in a guild far in advance of a game or server
release, and as such I can only offer observations, not experiences.  We
will look at each of these options in order and make some evaluation of
their potential strengths and weaknesses.<br />
<br />
Deciding to join a guild far in advance of release (anything up to a month
before the release of the game) holds a number of obvious advantages.
Coming in early in the life of a guild can allow one time to make a place
for oneself, both socially and in a hierarchical sense.  It also allows a
greater degree of influence over the environment of the guild, as well as
the plansmade before release.  This is not to say that you will be loved,
respected, made an officer and pretty much given free reign to implement as
you please, but it doesn't hurt to be one of the founding members of a
guild.  The downside of joining such a guild is that they can fall apart
before they begin.  It is possible that a guild formed many months in
advance of the release may actually not survive until the release of the
game itself.  Members may decide to not join the game, to join a different
guild or server, or the guild leadership may for whatever reason not form
the guild upon release.  (Note that I have no personal experience with a
guild formed this far in advance, thus my writings are conjectural in this
instance.)<br />
<br />
Making the decision to join a guild which is available a short time in
advance of release allows for adequate time to become integrated into the
guild as well as assist in post-release plannning, at the expense of perhaps
being a (slightly) later addition to the guild's roster.  Often the number
of guilds available closer to release is greater, thus giving a better
selection to the discerning guild-seeker.  There is still the risk of a
guild not actually being formed within the game however, for all the reasons
detailed above.<br />
<br />
Waiting until after release to join an official in-game guild is
advantageous for many obvious reasons.  While guilds come and go, the fact
that it exists in the game on a server you've choosen to play on is
definitely a big plus.  If the guild was not planned prior to release or
only had a small number of members, you may also experience the benefit of
being a founding member as well as having the opportunity to gain a position
of power within the organization.  Quickly joining a guild can have
downsides however, as it is not always evident how often your guildmates
will play and if their schedules will work well with yours.  It's also
difficult to determine in a short period of time what personality conflicts
might be present.  Always attempt be sure that you're joining a guild which
seems to suit your play style and schedule!<br />
<br />
Taking the long view and waiting until you've had some time to observe the
dynamics of various guilds on your server is obviously a wise decision for
the cautious player.  You will tend to avoid joining a guild which is
inappropriate to your play style and schedule, so long as you take some time
to research potential guilds and particularly their members.  A guild at the
heart is a collective of people, and spending time with those people can be
an excellent method of determining if a particular organization is right for
you.  The extra time allowed by the deference of the decision can give you
plenty of time to attend social events or raids hosted by the guild as well.
Many guilds will offer guest raid spots to unguilded players of an
appropriate power level in order to perhaps recruit them in the future.  The
disadvantages of being unguilded are myriad, of course.  Throughout your
waiting it may be more difficult to come across groups, discounted prices on
goods and services, or even general assistance.<br />
<br />
Once you have decided when you (think you) are going to join a guild, it's
important to take a look at what you personally want from a guild, as well
as what you have to offer.  If you're interested in acting out your role in
society and reciting poetry for the visitors to Qeynos, it might be a bad
plan for you to join a guild full of players whose main concern is how
quickly they can get the next level, shiny magic item or other tangible
reward.  Likewise, those who relish logging in to hack down as many critters
for as long as humanly possible may find it not so advantageous as they
might like to band together with a guild populated primarily by people
looking to spend most of their time bragging about killing orcs over a mug
of ale.  Finding people with playstyles and schedules is so important that I
couldn't even wait to address it in this section as you can seem from the
above.  Much grief may be spared simply by asking the right questions.<br />

<ul>
<li>
Do the other members play (or plan to at least) at the same general time as
you do?
</li>
<li>
Is the average power level of your fellow guild members likely to match
yours?
</li>
<li>
Does the guild take time to plan the kinds of events you are interested in?
</li>
<li>
Are the policies of the guild regarding leadership, looting, etc. in line
with those you favor?
</li>
</ul>
As long as you take a bit of time and do your research the chance of you
enjoying membership in one guild for a long period of time increase.  This
can be important when you consider the fact that people who move from guild
to guild may be viewed with caution or even distrust, particularly if they
were formerly members of an influential guild where something went badly.
This is not to say that you should be a member of one guild forever!  It is
simply that finding a good organization which suits your needs for a long
period of time is generally preferable to bouncing from place to place.<br />
<br />
Bearing what has been said previously, where do you find a guild when you
want it with the traits you're looking for?  Well, hopefully the Conclave of
Guilds will grow to provide an excellent place for all Everquest 2 players
to visit in regards to all guild-related information.  You can also check
other websites dedicated to the game, or even use a search engine to see
what you uncover!  Talking to friends with the same interest in this highly
anticipated sequel may yield some results.  There is also plenty of
opportunity to engage others in conversation regarding new guilds on chat
services as well as in newsgroups.<br />
<br />
</blockquote>
